External surfaces on Linux (GPUI patch 1 of docs/plans/20261014-gpui-frontend.md, the renderer half; the scene and
window half is build/patches/gpui-pre). Three parts, as docs/spikes/20261014-wpe-dmabuf.md found them necessary:
- device: the Vulkan device is opened through wgpu-hal's `open_with_callback` + `create_device_from_hal`, adding
  VK_EXT_image_drm_format_modifier and VK_EXT_queue_family_foreign (wgpu enables the external memory extensions where
  supported, never these); an adapter without them, without Vulkan 1.2 timeline semaphores or not on Vulkan opens as
  before and imports nothing. What the device imports (render node from VK_EXT_physical_device_drm, the formats and
  modifiers it copies from) is published.
- import: a dmabuf becomes a raw `VkImage` with DRM-modifier tiling and every memory plane's layout (non-disjoint:
  Intel's CCS keeps three planes in one object) on a dedicated allocation importing the fd, checked against the
  published capabilities first and never handed to wgpu (wgpu-core would make its first use an UNDEFINED transition,
  letting the driver discard the producer's pixels, and never acquires from a foreign queue family). Each new frame is
  acquired from VK_QUEUE_FAMILY_FOREIGN_EXT in GENERAL, copied into a wgpu texture (the buffer's snapshot, put into
  and found in COPY_DST through `CommandEncoder::transition_resources`) and released back, in a raw-only command
  buffer submitted with the frame; imports are cached per buffer and destroyed once the producer dropped it and the
  GPU finished the last copy.
- draw: a pipeline of its own (bindings 4-6 of group 1, clear of the macOS surface's) samples the snapshot within the
  content mask, padding alpha read as opaque, premultiplied texels blended for either target alpha mode; CPU pixels
  upload into a texture per surface, damaged rectangles only when the previous frame is the one uploaded last. Each
  submission that copies or first draws a frame signals a timeline semaphore; a thread reports the frames once the GPU
  reached it (vkWaitSemaphores, outside wgpu's locks: `Device::poll` would hold the fence lock `Queue::submit` needs),
  `Lost` on a lost device; a frame whose submission was abandoned reports `Discarded`.
scripts/vendor-gpui.sh applies this to the verified crates.io archive.

